unmask-nginx-external
=====================

For a host whose nginx no package installed: one built from source, by hand
or by a hosting panel that compiles its own.

unmask-plugin-nginx and unmask-web-nginx depend on "nginx".  On such a host
the package manager meets that dependency by installing the distribution's
nginx beside the one the host runs -- a second nginx, which takes the name
`nginx` on PATH and may take the ports.  This package provides "nginx" and
installs nothing but this file and its licence: installed first, and on
purpose, it tells the package manager that the host has an nginx already.

    apt install unmask-nginx-external unmask unmask-plugin-nginx unmask-web-nginx
    dnf install unmask-nginx-external unmask unmask-plugin-nginx unmask-web-nginx

What unmask does with an nginx built from source
------------------------------------------------

It edits nothing of that nginx.  The module and the files nginx has to read
are kept in directories of unmask's own, and the packages name two lines to
add to that nginx's nginx.conf, once:

    include /usr/share/unmask/nginx/modules-enabled/*.conf;    as its first line
    include /usr/share/unmask/nginx/conf.d/*.conf;             inside http { }

Both are wildcard includes: nginx reads them as nothing when unmask has
taken its module out or has been removed, so they never stop it from
starting.  `unmask doctor` says which of the two is still missing.

The nginx has to be one of the versions the plugin bundles a module for,
and built --with-compat (a hosting panel: the custom parameters of its nginx
build).  The plugin says so, in words, when it is not.

When that nginx is built again -- another version, other options -- run

    sh /usr/share/unmask/plugin/place-module.sh

before it is started: it places the module for the new build, or takes the
module out when there is none for it, so that nginx starts either way.

Do not install this package on a host whose nginx came from a package.
